  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: c18dce35c4f218942974316577a84ef5, type: 3}
  m_Name: Scene12_Context
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::OhmsLaw.SceneAgentContext
  sceneId: Scene12
  sceneTitle: Discover Resistance
  askWattsonEnabled: 1
  covered:
  - "Everything from Scenes 5 to 11: voltage (V, volt), current (I, ampere), the
    five readings, V \u221D I for the nichrome wire at constant temperature, the
    straight-line graph through the origin."
  - 'The 3D comparison showed two wires of the same length, thickness and temperature
    at the same voltage: inside a metal the atoms sit in a lattice and the free electrons
    thread through it; in copper they slip past with few collisions (big current),
    in nichrome they scatter and lose headway (less current). Each collision hands
    energy to the lattice as heat. Resistance limits the current; the charges are
    not destroyed.'
  - The material of a conductor opposes the flow of current. That opposition is electrical
    resistance, symbol R. Nichrome has the higher resistance.
  - "For the nichrome wire XY, V/I is the same for every reading (1.0 \xF7 0.20 =
    5, 2.0 \xF7 0.40 = 5, ...). V \u221D I means V = kI, so V/I = k, and that constant
    k is the resistance R: V = IR."
  - "The slope of the V-I graph (V vertical, I horizontal) is \u0394V/\u0394I = 2.0
    V \xF7 0.40 A = 5, the resistance."
  - "Resistance is measured in ohms (\u03A9). 1 \u03A9 = 1 V / 1 A: one ohm when
    one volt produces one ampere. This wire: 5 \u03A9."
  doNotSpoil:
  - The name 'Ohm's law' and V = IR as the named law (Scene 13)
  - The Christmas-lights answer
  faq: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 64a1b90ece9df0241bbf17844fdc03c3, type: 2}
